<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="95128326">&lt;p&gt;
    Estimating the required effort to deliver the outlined work (in either User Stories, features, Epics, etc.)
    is&amp;nbsp;fundamental to the success of Agile delivery as it directly informs the planning process. By understanding how
    much work can be completed within the course of a sprint (velocity) and the overall estimate for the entire Product
    Backlog, it is possible to estimate how many sprints will be needed to address all of the work.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    There are two primary means of estimating, relativity based sizing and hours/days, the pros and cons for each are as
    follows:&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table title=&quot;&quot; style=&quot;HEIGHT: 178px; WIDTH: 566px&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;2&quot; width=&quot;566&quot; border=&quot;1&quot;&gt;
    &lt;tbody&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Ideal Days&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Story Points&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Intuitive&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Somewhat
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Encourages collaborative estimating&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Somewhat
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Sufficient precision&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Estimate specific to person responsible for executing the work&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                No
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Risk of confusing duration and effort&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Yes
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                No
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Supports accurate release planning&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Somewhat
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                Mostly
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
        &lt;tr&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &lt;strong&gt;Validates capacity allocation per individual team member&lt;/strong&gt;
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                No
            &lt;/td&gt;
            &lt;td&gt;
                &amp;nbsp;No
            &lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    To be effective, only one form of estimation should be chosen and consistently applied. The &lt;a class=&quot;elementLink&quot;
    href=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/guidances/guidelines/affinity_est_tech_381AADB4.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_h7etQESuEeiASMlqgAcI7A&quot;&gt;Affinity Estimation Technique&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;is often used when the Product Backlog is being
    initially estimated and accurate estimates are not yet required. Estimates are then refined via the &lt;a
    class=&quot;elementLink&quot;
    href=&quot;./../../../practice.mgmt.proj.agile_pm.base/guidances/guidelines/planning_poker_tech_24B7DD8A.html&quot;
    guid=&quot;_Lc8YwESwEeiASMlqgAcI7A&quot;&gt;Planning Poker Technique&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;typically during the sprint planning meeting.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/strong&gt;It is important to consider that while estimation&amp;nbsp;techniques&amp;nbsp;may be the&amp;nbsp;same, values in story
    points or ideal days may differ across projects or teams due to maturity and experience of resources. The information
    provided here should serve as a guide to evaluate and understand the various types of estimation techniques available.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Also keep in mind that changes to estimates cannot occur during a sprint once the team has begun working on a
    particular Backlog Item. Instead, changes to Backlog Item estimates must be made during sprint planning or product
    backlog refinement, before the Backlog Item is included in the Sprint Backlog
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
